---
/**
 * 手写页（迁移自 en/guides/llm-api-cost-examples/index.html，源码即产物 → Astro 源文件）。
 * 语言：en；标记注入区已改为 frontmatter 函数调用（同一数据源/同一公式）；
 * 联盟/广告占位注释原样保留，构建后由 placement 状态机重写。
 */
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../../../components/placements/AdSlot.astro';
import { getSiteData } from '../../../../lib/data';
import ScenarioTables from '../../../../components/guide/ScenarioTables.astro';
import SensitivityTable from '../../../../components/guide/SensitivityTable.astro';

const { models, fx } = await getSiteData();
---

<Layout title={"LLM API Monthly Cost Scenarios | Reflex Hub"}
  desc={"Monthly cost estimates for three typical workloads — chatbot/customer service, RAG knowledge-base Q&A and coding agents — with explicit token-usage assumptions and per-model calculations from official list prices; check, adapt or replace every parameter."}
  ogDescription={"Chatbot, RAG and coding-agent scenarios with explicit assumptions and per-model estimates."}
  canonicalPath="/en/guides/llm-api-cost-examples/"
  active="guides"
  crumb={"/ <a href=\"../../\" style=\"color:inherit\">Guides</a> / Cost scenarios"}>
<section class="hero">
      <h1>LLM API monthly cost scenarios</h1>
      <p class="tagline">Chatbot · RAG knowledge base · coding agent</p>
    </section>

    <p class="lede">
      <strong>The problem</strong>: a unit-price table cannot tell you what your business will spend per month — cost is
      determined by scenario parameters. <strong>The answer</strong>: the same model lineup produces completely different
      monthly cost structures in three typical scenarios — high-frequency short interactions (customer service) amplify
      call count, long-context injection (RAG) amplifies input and cache, and long outputs (coding agent) amplify the
      output price. Each scenario below states explicit token-usage assumptions and per-model calculations from official
      list prices, so you can verify or replace any parameter.
    </p>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>1. Estimation method (shared by all three scenarios)</h2>
      <div style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9">
        <p>
          · Monthly cost = daily calls × 30 × (input per call ÷ 1,000,000 × effective input price + output per call ÷ 1,000,000 × output price);<br>
          · Effective input price = hit rate × cache-hit price + (1 − hit rate) × miss price; models without a published cache price use the miss price;<br>
          · Time-based models use off-peak rates; input-length-tiered models pick the tier from the scenario's per-call input;<br>
          · USD models keep their $ list price; monthly costs are shown converted to CNY at the reference rate — indicative only;<br>
          · Step-by-step derivation in <a href="../llm-api-cost/">How to calculate LLM API costs</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      
<ScenarioTables models={models} fx={fx} en />

    </section>

    <AdSlot name="guide-mid" />
    <section class="panel">
      <h2>2. Comparing scenarios (factual observations)</h2>
      <div style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9">
        <p>
          · <strong>The RAG bill lives on the input side</strong>: injecting 10,000 tokens per call puts input at about 70–90% of the monthly cost — the cache price and hit rate become decisive. Make the system prompt and knowledge-base prefix stable so hits are billed at the cache price;<br>
          · <strong>The coding-agent bill lives on the output side</strong>: long patches and explanations inflate output tokens; switching to a model with a lower <em>output</em> price beats switching to one with a cheaper input;<br>
          · <strong>Call count amplifies the customer-service bill</strong>: the cheapest lightweight tier stays an order of magnitude cheaper at high frequency — as long as your per-call input stays inside the lower tier;<br>
          · Common to all three: off-peak halving applies to time-based models — deferrable jobs (nightly batches, asynchronous RAG rebuilds) gain a deterministic discount in off-peak windows.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>3. Sensitivity example: how cache hit rate changes the RAG bill</h2>
      <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9">
        Using scenario 2 (RAG, 300 calls/day, 10,000 input / 500 output tokens, off-peak rates), hold every other
        assumption fixed and vary only the cache hit rate to see how each model's estimate moves on the same basis.
        The table is generated by the build script with the same formula — it is <strong>an illustration of the order of
        magnitude of this single variable</strong>, not an industry survey or user statistics.
      </p>
      
<SensitivityTable models={models} fx={fx} en />

    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>4. Data sources and limitations</h2>
      <div style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9">
        <p>
          · All unit prices come from this site's single source models.json (official list prices, each with a source link); verification dates appear in each table; price-verification time ≠ page-content update time, and re-verification without price changes does not update page dates (see the <a href="../../pricing-methodology/">pricing methodology</a>);<br>
          · Scenario parameters (call counts, token volumes, hit rates) are <strong>illustrative assumptions, not measured statistics</strong> — they demonstrate cost structure; replace them with your own log data and recompute;<br>
          · Estimates exclude free tiers, resource packs, subscription discounts and limited-time promotions and are not actual invoices; tiers with unpublished rates honestly show "cannot estimate".
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>5. Next steps</h2>
      <div style="font-size:14px;line-height:2">
        <p>
          · Recompute with your parameters: <a href="../../cost/">cost calculator</a> (any usage and hit-rate combination);<br>
          · Whole-market differences at fixed assumptions: <a href="../llm-api-pricing-comparison/">pricing comparison guide</a>;<br>
          · Full profiles with official sources: <a href="../../models/">model library</a>; two candidates via <a href="../../compare/">model comparison</a>;<br>
          · Back to the <a href="../">guide directory</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
</Layout>
